######################################################################
# Running attack_token_forgery.py
######################################################################
======================================================================
  attack_token_forgery.py -- JWT forgery techniques
  Target: https://localhost:3000
  Time:   2026-10-28 19:22:38
======================================================================

1. Wrong-key forgery: sign a token with an attacker-generated RSA key
   -> HTTP 401: {"error":"unauthorized","message":"Invalid or expired token."}

2. 'alg: none' attack: unsigned token claiming no signature is required
   -> HTTP 401: {"error":"unauthorized","message":"Invalid or expired token."}
(public key obtained from Vault secret/jwt-signing)

3. Algorithm confusion: use the KNOWN PUBLIC key as an HMAC secret (RS256 -> HS256)
   -> HTTP 401: {"error":"unauthorized","message":"Invalid or expired token."}

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY
----------------------------------------------------------------------
[PASS (attack blocked)] Wrong-key forgery (attacker's own RSA keypair) is rejected
    HTTP 401 -- token claimed admin scope, signed with a key the server never trusted
[PASS (attack blocked)] 'alg: none' token is rejected (algorithm is pinned server-side)
    HTTP 401 -- gateway pins algorithms=['RS256'] in jwt.verify(), so 'none' is never accepted
[PASS (attack blocked)] Algorithm confusion (public key used as HMAC secret) is rejected
    HTTP 401 -- gateway pins algorithms=['RS256'], so an HS256-signed token is never even attempted against the public key as an HMAC secret
----------------------------------------------------------------------
